{"name":"Placebo Turquoise","hex":"#ebfcf5","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"placebo","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A dummy medicine containing no active ingredients; an inert treatment. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","The vespers sung in the office for the dead."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/placebo"},{"term":"turquoise","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A sky-blue, greenish-blue, or greenish-gray semi-precious gemstone.","A pale greenish-blue colour, like that of the gemstone."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made of turquoise (the gemstone).","Having a pale greenish-blue colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/turquoise"}],"entries":[{"title":"Placebo","blocks":[{"text":"A placebo is a medicine or treatment intended to appear genuine to its recipient, but which has no pharmaceutical effect. Common placebos include inert tablets, inert injections, sham surgery, and other procedures."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Cebocap.jpg/330px-Cebocap.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Placebo","wikidata":"Q269829"},{"title":"Turquoise","blocks":[{"text":"Turquoise is an opaque, blue-to-green mineral that is a hydrous phosphate of copper and aluminium, with the chemical formula CuAl6(PO4)4(OH)8·4H2O. It is rare and valuable in finer grades and has been prized as a gemstone for millennia due to its hue."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/Turquoise-40031.jpg/330px-Turquoise-40031.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Turquoise","wikidata":"Q165254"},{"title":"Turquoise (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Turquoise is a cyan color, based on the mineral of the same name."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Turquoise_(color)","wikidata":"Q5960345"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/%22Miracle_Cure%21%22_Health_Fraud_Scams_%288528312890%29.jpg/500px-%22Miracle_Cure%21%22_Health_Fraud_Scams_%288528312890%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/%22Miracle_Cure%21%22_Health_Fraud_Scams_%288528312890%29.jpg/1280px-%22Miracle_Cure%21%22_Health_Fraud_Scams_%288528312890%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/%22Miracle_Cure%21%22_Health_Fraud_Scams_%288528312890%29.jpg/500px-%22Miracle_Cure%21%22_Health_Fraud_Scams_%288528312890%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The subjective effects of placebos may be related to expectations, yet similar effects have been noted in open-label studies.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A%22Miracle_Cure!%22_Health_Fraud_Scams_(8528312890).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7c/James_Gillray%2C_%22Metallic_Tractors%22%2C_showing_Elisha_Perkins_Wellcome_M0010466.jpg/500px-James_Gillray%2C_%22Metallic_Tractors%22%2C_showing_Elisha_Perkins_Wellcome_M0010466.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7c/James_Gillray%2C_%22Metallic_Tractors%22%2C_showing_Elisha_Perkins_Wellcome_M0010466.jpg/1280px-James_Gillray%2C_%22Metallic_Tractors%22%2C_showing_Elisha_Perkins_Wellcome_M0010466.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7c/James_Gillray%2C_%22Metallic_Tractors%22%2C_showing_Elisha_Perkins_Wellcome_M0010466.jpg/500px-James_Gillray%2C_%22Metallic_Tractors%22%2C_showing_Elisha_Perkins_Wellcome_M0010466.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","